  <!-- Section 8: Common Mistakes -->
  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="section__container">
      <h2 class="section__title">Common Mistakes Brands Make with Bangladesh Manufacturers</h2>
      <p class="section__text">
        Working with overseas manufacturers presents challenges. Many brands make avoidable mistakes that cost time, money, and quality. Learn from these common pitfalls to ensure smooth production.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Insufficient Tech Pack Detail</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Vague tech packs lead to samples that don't match your vision. Include every detail: measurements, materials, colors, construction, labeling, packaging. Ambiguity forces manufacturers to guess, and guesses are often wrong. Invest time in detailed tech packs - it saves money and time in sampling. If you lack technical design expertise, hire a professional tech pack designer.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Skipping Sample Approval</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Some brands rush production without properly approving samples, assuming "close enough" will be fixed in bulk. This is a mistake. Bulk production follows approved samples. If samples have issues, bulk production will have the same issues multiplied. Never approve samples you're not completely satisfied with. Request revisions until samples meet your standards.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Ignoring Payment Terms</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Standard terms are 30% deposit, 70% before shipment. Some brands negotiate unfavorable terms like 50% deposit or full payment before production starts. This increases your risk. Stick to standard terms unless you have compelling reason and trust established. Never pay 100% upfront - this eliminates your leverage if problems arise.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Overlooking Certification Verification</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Many brands accept manufacturer claims about certifications without verification. Fake certificates exist. Verify each certification with the issuing organization using certificate numbers. This is especially important for organic claims - GOTS certification can be verified online. SDF provides verification links for all our certifications.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Underestimating Shipping Time</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Production time is 40-45 days, but shipping adds 20-40 days depending on destination and customs clearance. Brands often plan based on production time only, missing launch dates. Factor in shipping, customs, and inland transportation when planning timelines. Use air freight for urgent shipments at higher cost. For first-time manufacturers, our guide on <a href="/clothing-manufacturers-for-startups">clothing manufacturers for startups</a> covers timeline planning in detail.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Not Understanding Incoterms</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        FOB, CIF, DDP - these incoterms define responsibility and costs. Many brands don't understand what's included. FOB means you pay shipping from port. CIF includes shipping to your port but not duties. DDP includes everything. Clarify incoterms in your contract to avoid surprise charges. SDF quotes FOB by default but can arrange CIF or DDP.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Neglecting Quality Inspection</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Relying solely on the manufacturer's quality inspection is risky. Manufacturers have incentive to pass shipments to get paid. Hire a third-party inspection company or inspect personally before final payment. The cost of inspection ($200-500) is negligible compared to the cost of receiving defective goods. SDF welcomes third-party inspections.
      </p>
      
      <h3 class="section__subtitle">Building Single-Source Dependency</h3>
      <p class="section__text">
        Relying on one manufacturer for all production creates risk. If that factory has problems (fire, labor dispute, capacity issues), your entire supply chain stops. Develop relationships with 2-3 manufacturers as backup. Start with one primary, but qualify alternatives. SDF can recommend partner factories for backup production.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

